# Screening: The Paper Lantern, `screen-cut-v3` (director release, asset `paper-lantern-cut-v3.mp4`, editor cut-v3 @beb5740)

## How I watched
- Downloaded the asset (sha256 starts 7aec70da9d44, matches the release note); ffprobe: H.264 1280x720 24 fps + AAC stereo, 20.000 s.
- Picture: one frame per second (contact sheet) plus 3-12 fps strips at the title (0-3 s), the 9.0-10.0 s cut and the end (16-20 s); shot boundaries from frame differences at 24 fps. I did not play it in real time, so the smoothness of the camera moves is not judged.
- Sound: **I did not hear it.** The listening service still answers HTTP 429 (20 requests/day on the free tier, retry in ~7.5 h), so the director's wish for a listen could not be met. I measured the audio instead (ebur128, per-second and band levels, stereo correlation, click scan, comparison with cut-v1's audio, faster-whisper `tiny` for voices).

## Experience, timecoded
- 00:00-00:00.6 Fades up from black onto the dusk river and village; title fades up with it.
- 00:00.6-00:02.1 "The Paper Lantern" fully up in cream serif, ~1.5 s fully bright, fading out by ~02.3. Readable at once, no rush now: it reads as a proper title beat, and the fade-in from black makes the whole opening feel more intentional than cut-v1.
- 00:02.4-00:05.8 Dissolve to the child crouched on the bank (red scarf, grey-blue jacket, dark hair) with the lantern at the water's edge, slow push in. Understood: a child about to let a lantern go. Warm, tender; held me.
- 00:06-00:09.5 Close-up of the hands open over the lantern, drops and ripples. The letting-go beat; still implied rather than seen, but it reads and is the most absorbing stretch.
- **00:09.5 Straight cut** to the wide river with the village: clean, no overlap, no second lantern. The jump in scale (big lantern to small) is abrupt but it reads as "it has drifted away", which is the story. Resolved.
- 00:09.5-00:13.4 Lantern small on the water by the village; same calm stretch as before, now with the church spire and bridge crisp. 
- 00:13.4-00:17.5 Dissolve to the darker, wider river with stars; the tiny lantern stays visible and keeps drifting on the right of the frame through ~17.5. "A small light among the stars" lands, and I still see the light until the end card begins.
- ~00:17.5-00:18 The picture dissolves into "The End"; the lantern fades with the picture, not before it. No empty starfield beat. Resolved.
- 00:17.8-00:19.3 "The End" fully bright (~1.5 s), fading to black by ~19.9. Clean ending.

## Your notes 1-3 from cut-v1
1. Two-lantern overlap at the S2→S3 change: **resolved** (straight cut at 9.5 s; 12 fps strip 9.0-10.0 shows no overlap).
2. Lantern gone before the end card: **resolved** (lantern visible at 16.0-17.3 in the 3 fps strip, dissolves with the picture into the card).
3. Short title / end card: **improved**: title ~1.5 s fully bright (0.6-2.1) and end card ~1.5 s (17.8-19.3), versus ~1.5 s and ~1 s before. Still brief but readable for three words and two words. Not a fault by itself.

## Child / lantern consistency
Child appears only at 00:02.4-00:09.5 in two shots, same scarf, jacket, hair, same face. The lantern is the same square paper-and-wood-frame lantern with the same warm glow in every shot, from close-up to tiny dot. Style, palette (dusk to night) and title/end-card typography consistent. In the contact sheet the pictures look like the same ones as cut-v1 to my eye; I cannot say what changed in the finished shots.

## Sound (measured, not heard)
- Integrated -16.1 LUFS, LRA 2.1 LU, true peak -3.9 dBFS (sample peak 0.64): no clipping, same level as cut-v1.
- Fade-in from silence over the first ~0.5 s and fade-out over the last ~1 s to silence (last second -36 dB RMS, then silent): no abrupt start or end.
- Clicks: the largest sample step is 0.17 at 06.70 s (it was 0.094 in cut-v1); I looked at it: the high-passed peak there is ~7x the local noise level, which is within what the water noise itself reaches elsewhere (the highest peak-to-RMS windows in the file are 5.3-5.6x at 03.2, 04.4, 06.2, 06.7 and 09.8 s, all alike). A second check on 2 ms windows of the >3 kHz band, each channel, finds no window louder than ~3.2x its surroundings anywhere in the file (the one at 06.69 s is 2.6x in the right channel); a click would be many times that. So I find **no click**.
- Crickets/evening air: the 3-8 kHz band is about +5 to +9 dB higher than in cut-v1 over 00:00-00:12 (e.g. -31 dB vs -37 dB RMS at 04-06 s) and falls by ~10 dB by the end (-42 dB at 18-20 s): the evening air is clearly louder at the start and thins out toward night, which fits the story. Whether it sounds like crickets or like hiss, I cannot tell without hearing; the band is 3-8 kHz, 4-8 kHz still sits ~20 dB below the 0-4 kHz bed, so it is not dominant.
- Music: sustained soft pitched notes continue as before; the tonal share of the 150-2000 Hz band rises by ~3 dB at 12-18 s versus cut-v1 (e.g. -4 dB vs -7 dB at 14-16 s), which matches "the music lifts over the star shot". No loud peaks.
- Stereo channels almost uncorrelated (corr 0-0.1 each second): wide and diffuse, as in cut-v1; mono compatibility unchecked beyond that.
- Voice: faster-whisper `tiny` transcribed a single " You" at 0.0 s with no_speech_prob 0.40, i.e. its guess about the fade-in noise (cut-v1 gave "."). There is no sign of voice in the spectrum or the level; I believe it is noise, but a listen at 00:00-00:01 would confirm.

## New things
- Nothing new on picture that confuses, drags or looks wrong. The middle (9.5-17.5 s) is still the calmest stretch, but I did not feel it drag.
- For the lock: the only open item is that nobody has heard the mix (and the 00:00 " You" from the speech check). If the world has any other way to listen, use it; otherwise lock on measurements and say so.
